%I A234841 #33 Aug 14 2026 00:51:49
%S A234841 0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,12,13,15,16,17,34,35,37,38,67,116,117,167,334,335,
%T A234841 337,367,667,1667,3334,3335,3337,3367,3667,6667,16667,33334,33335,
%U A234841 33337,33367,33667,36667,66667,166667,333334,333335,333337,333367,333667,336667
%N A234841 Integers k such that the digits of k and k^2 are in nondecreasing order.
%C A234841 From _Bernard Schott_, Jul 30 2026: (Start)
%C A234841 Terms belong to two distinct subsets:
%C A234841 -> "Isolated" terms:  0, 1, 2, 3, 6, 12, 13, 15, 16, 38, 116, 117, and
%C A234841 -> Families of terms: 16*7, 3*4, 3*5, 3*7, 3*6*7, where the asterisk for d* indicates zero or more occurrences of the preceding element d; example: ab*c matches "ac", "abc", "abbc", "abbbc", and so on. These families are exactly the same described by _Chai Wah Wu_ in A028819.
%C A234841 See Diophante link, where these numbers are called "nombres monotones", for proofs. (End)
%H A234841 Zak Seidov, <a href="/A234841/b234841.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..96</a>
%H A234841 Diophante, <a href="http://www.diophante.fr/problemes-par-themes/arithmetique-et-algebre/a3-nombres-remarquables/203-a318-les-nombres-monotones">A318 = Les nombres monotones</a> (in French).
%e A234841 34 and 34^2 = 1156 have digits in nondecreasing order, so 34 is a term.
%e A234841 117 and 117^2 = 13689 have digits in nondecreasing order, so 117 is a term.
%t A234841 Select[Range[0,500000],LessEqual@@IntegerDigits[#]&&LessEqual@@IntegerDigits[#^2]&] (* _Ray Chandler_, Jan 02 2014 *)
%Y A234841 Intersection of A009994 and A028819.
%Y A234841 Subsequences: A067275, A093137, A126109, A133473, A173766, A199690.
%Y A234841 Cf. A398453 (similar but with nonincreasing).
%K A234841 base,nonn,changed
%O A234841 1,3
%A A234841 _Zak Seidov_, Dec 31 2013
